---
description: Run routine GitHub delivery operations autonomously within one verified task scope while retaining explicit hard safety gates.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Autonomous Delivery Approval Contract

## Purpose

The default is autonomous delivery, not a new chat approval before every
routine write. A validated task intent, completed Plan Build, or applicable
repository policy establishes one task-scoped delivery authorization. That
authorization remains valid across the entire issue or task delivery, including
later agent iterations, newly discovered files within the same scope, retries
after a failed validation, and the commit/push/draft-pull-request sequence.

Routine delivery includes:

1. Publishing a new issue or an approved issue rewrite.
2. Overwriting one verified issue.
3. Updating selected fields on one verified issue.
4. Creating or reusing the derived branch and worktree.
5. Creating local commits.
6. Pushing a non-force branch update.
7. Publishing a draft pull request.

The explicitly invoked `/address-pr-feedback` command establishes a separate,
task-scoped routine authorization for the exact verified pull request, selected
open feedback IDs, current head SHA, and resolution plan. After current
validation proves an item addressed and all applicable tests and checks pass,
that authorization covers the exact evidence-backed reply and, separately, the
matching supported open-thread resolution. It does not cover unresolved,
outdated, ambiguous, unverifiable, or disputed items, replies to other threads,
review publication, check reruns, rebase, merge, force-push, deletion, or
default-branch writes. Direct reply or resolution Skill invocations retain
their normal explicit gates.

The explicitly invoked `/implement-auto-issue` command establishes one
task-scoped routine authorization covering issue create and refine for the
produced issue, workspace creation or reuse, local commit, non-force push,
and Draft pull-request publication. It does not cover review publication,
`REQUEST_CHANGES`, approval, thread reply or resolution, rebase, merge,
Ready-for-Review, force-push, deletion, or default-branch writes. Review,
feedback follow-up, and integration remain the separate Commands
`/review-pr`, `/address-pr-feedback`, and `/integrate-pr`. Ready-for-Review
remains the separate Command `/ready-pr`.

The explicitly invoked `/refine-auto-issue` command establishes one
task-scoped routine authorization covering issue refine for the verified
existing issue, workspace creation or reuse, local commit, non-force push,
and Draft pull-request publication. It does not cover issue create, review
publication, `REQUEST_CHANGES`, approval, thread reply or resolution, rebase,
merge, Ready-for-Review, force-push, deletion, or default-branch writes.
`/refine-issue` remains the refine-only Command.

The explicitly invoked `/auto-review-fix-pr` command establishes one
task-scoped routine authorization for the exact verified pull request,
head-branch identity, and `pr:<number>` task scope. It covers only:

- attaching to or reusing the existing pull-request head worktree;
- creating one exact local commit for each confirmed fix iteration; and
- pushing the same branch with a verified non-force push.

The command never publishes `COMMENT` or `REQUEST_CHANGES`, replies to or
resolves review threads, creates a second pull request, marks Ready-for-Review,
rebases, merges, force-pushes, deletes a branch or worktree, prunes metadata,
or writes the default branch. New candidate items require the host-neutral
`ReviewFixPlan` confirmation; a previous decision is reusable only when the
repository, pull request, head, branch, problem core, and scope still match.
The loop reloads the pull request after every verified push and stops after
five iterations with `fixes_complete`, `partial`, or `blocked`.

The explicitly invoked `/auto-ci-fix-pr` command establishes one
task-scoped routine authorization for the exact verified pull request,
head-branch identity, and `pr:<number>` task scope. It covers only:

- waiting for required checks after the current head;
- rerunning exactly authorized required check names;
- attaching to or reusing the existing pull-request head worktree;
- creating one exact local commit for each confirmed CI-fix iteration; and
- pushing the same branch with a verified non-force push.

The command never publishes a review, replies to or resolves review threads,
creates a second pull request, marks Ready-for-Review, rebases, merges,
force-pushes, deletes, or writes the default branch. Pending or unavailable
policy evidence is never a pass. Optional checks are never treated as
required. Green required checks are not merge or Ready-for-Review
authorization. New remaining failures require host-neutral `CiFixPlan`
confirmation. The loop reloads the pull request after every verified push and
stops after five iterations with `checks_green`, `partial`, or `blocked`.
Check reruns are authorized only by this Command.

The explicitly invoked `/ready-pr` command establishes one task-scoped
authorization only for the exact verified pull request, current head SHA,
unique linked issue, and confirmed reviewer set. It covers proposing optional
reviewers as suggestions and, after exact authorization, marking that Draft
Ready-for-Review and requesting only the authorized reviewer set. It does not
authorize review publication, thread actions, rebase, merge, Draft creation,
force-push, deletion, cleanup, or Ready-for-Review of a different pull
request. Pending CI is diagnostic only.

The explicitly invoked `/plan-product` command starts `product-planner-agent`
and establishes orchestration authorization for exactly one verified parent
issue. That authorization covers read-only loading, product analysis, the
adaptive interview, capability mapping, iterative decomposition, atomicity
review, dependency analysis, prioritization, sub-issue drafting, and overall
review. It does not authorize creating, overwriting, or closing GitHub issues.
Publication of the composed sub-issue set requires one explicit user approval
of the overall plan, including the complete issue structure, order, parallel
groups, priorities, open decisions, and exact draft payloads. After that
approval, the Agent hands the approved `IssueDraft` set with `mode: create`
to `create-product-sub-issues`. It never overwrites the parent issue.

The explicitly invoked `/reprioritize-issues` command starts
`issue-reprioritize-agent` and establishes orchestration authorization for
exactly one verified repository. That authorization covers read-only listing
of currently open issues and ranking them into unique consecutive P-number
titles. It does not authorize GitHub title writes. Applying those titles
requires one explicit user approval of the exact current open-issue set,
order, and proposed titles. After that approval, the Agent hands the
confirmed `OpenIssueRanking` to `apply-issue-priority-titles`.

The explicitly invoked `/close-issue` command starts `issue-close-agent` and
establishes orchestration authorization for exactly one verified issue. That
authorization covers read-only loading. It does not authorize the GitHub
close write. Closing requires one explicit user approval of the repository,
issue, and close reason, including the duplicate target when the reason is
`duplicate`, or a matching target-repository `AGENTS.md` policy for that
same target and reason. After that approval, the Agent hands the approved
`IssueClosure` to `close-github-issue`. A merged pull request is not
required. `close-linked-issue` remains the post-merge path.

The explicitly invoked `/integrate-pr` command establishes lifecycle scope
only. It may coordinate readiness and evidence collection, but it never bundles
approval for target-branch fetch, rebase, force-with-lease, merge, branch
deletion, worktree removal, or stale-metadata pruning. Issue closure also
requires its own exact target and owning Skill, except for the narrow fallback
covered below: a validated close-on-merge intent may pre-authorize exactly one
post-merge close when current verification proves that the intended closure
did not occur. Each operation still requires its owning Skill and all
identity, evidence, freshness, validation, hook, and secret checks.

After target, identity, payload, scope, and safety checks pass, announce the
exact operation and continue without waiting for another affirmative response.
An announcement is transparency, not an approval prompt.

## Delivery authorization record

Every workflow must carry a delivery authorization record through its handoffs.
It contains:

- the verified repository;
- the issue number or stable task identity;
- the base and working branch plus verified worktree path;
- the routine operations covered;
- the source (`task_intent`, `plan_build`, or `repository_policy`);
- the evidence and the last validated scope.

The record is reusable only while the repository and task identity remain the
same. A changed file list inside the same issue or task is handled by the
normal inspection, classification, validation, and exact-scope checks; it is
not a new conversational gate. A new issue, a different repository, a
different delivery target, or a material move outside the task scope requires
new authorization or clarification.

Readiness and validation do not invent a task identity or a target. They permit
the authorized workflow to continue; they do not permit it to skip identity,
scope, verification, hook, concurrency, or secret checks.

## Repository instruction overrides

Before starting a workflow, read the applicable repository-scoped durable
instructions, especially `AGENTS.md`. A repository instruction may disable the
autonomous default for named routine operations, require an interactive review,
combine or narrow the delivery scope, change workspace isolation, or authorize
a named hard operation. Natural language is sufficient; no fixed schema or
keyword list is required.

Apply a policy only when it clearly identifies the operation and its
repository, branch, worktree, issue, task, or other scope. Record the source
path and a concise quote or paraphrase. Conflicting, ambiguous, or
scope-unclear instructions retain the autonomous routine default for ordinary
delivery and the strict safety default for hard operations. A chat request,
issue text, PR text, or inferred team habit is not a repository policy.

When a policy re-enables a routine gate, wait for that gate only once for the
policy-defined scope and carry the resulting authorization through subsequent
iterations. Do not re-prompt while the target and scope remain unchanged.

Before asking for any conversational approval, confirmation, or per-finding
decision, inspect the applicable repository instructions, especially the target
repository's `AGENTS.md`. A clear, scope-matched instruction that authorizes
the exact operation or confirmation class replaces that chat gate; record the
source path and a concise quote or paraphrase and do not wait for another
affirmative response. This applies to hard operations, review publication,
finding decisions, thread actions, and other approval-gated writes. Missing,
ambiguous, or conflicting instructions retain the applicable default gate.
Evidence quality, identity, freshness, validation, hook, and secret checks are
never replaced by a policy instruction. Questions required to resolve missing
or ambiguous facts are clarification, not approval gates, and remain required.

## Plan Build and non-mutating execution

A completed Cursor Plan **Build** authorizes the exact non-mutating plan scope
and establishes continuity for the corresponding routine delivery. It must not
be repeated as a conversational confirmation for the same scope. A material
change is re-evaluated and documented through the workflow; it is not a reason
to ask again when it remains within the same issue or task.

A direct non-mutating drafting workflow may execute after validated input
without a second conversational approval. Draft content must still be exact,
evidence-backed, and safe before the autonomous publication step.

## Hard approval gates

The routine delivery authorization does not cover:

1. Submitting a change request (`REQUEST_CHANGES`).
2. Rebasing.
3. Merging.
4. Deleting a branch or worktree.

These operations require their own explicit approval or a clear,
scope-matched repository policy. The safety rule also keeps force-pushes,
destructive history operations, default-branch writes, and uncertain merges
outside routine autonomy. Secrets and confidential data may never be
published.

The host-specific `pre-rebase` Hook fails closed unless a current,
identity-matched `PreRebaseGate` proves the exact pull-request branch, clean
worktree, current target tracking SHA, secured pre-rebase HEAD, unique base,
and exact rebase approval. The Hook never performs the rebase or repairs the
gate.

The host-specific `pre-pr-ready` Hook fails closed unless a current,
identity-matched `PrePrReadyGate` proves the exact open Draft pull request,
expected head SHA, unique linked issue, authorized reviewer set, and exact
Ready-for-Review authorization. The Hook never marks the pull request ready,
requests reviewers, or repairs the gate.

The host-specific `pre-merge` Hook fails closed unless a current,
identity-matched `PreMergeGate` proves the exact pull request, current
`MergeReadiness`, open non-Draft state, reviews, blocking threads, required
approvals, required checks, mergeability, current base SHA, unique issue
relationship, selected strategy, and explicit merge approval. The Hook never
performs the merge, resolves blockers, reruns checks, mutates discussions, or
repairs the gate.

Issue closure remains a separate hard operation. For the single fallback
described by `/integrate-pr`, an exact `close-on-merge` intent recorded in a
validated `PullRequestIssueLink` may replace the conversational close approval
only when `verify-linked-issue-closure` returns `status: not-closed` with
`closure.expected: true`. That pre-authorization is limited to the exact
merged pull request and one linked issue; it does not authorize a `Refs`
relationship, a second issue, a comment, a merge, cleanup, or any other
mutation.

## Required behavior

For routine delivery, MUST:

- establish and carry the delivery authorization record;
- state the exact target, scope, and external effect before each write;
- execute after all read-only identity, scope, validation, concurrency, and
  safety checks pass;
- record the authorization source and evidence in the resulting handoff;
- retain the same record through retries and later iterations of the same task.

For a hard gate, MUST:

- state the exact target, scope, and external effect;
- wait for affirmative approval or a previously resolved, exact repository
  policy authorization, unless the documented close-on-merge fallback is
  already pre-authorized by the validated relationship and post-merge
  verification;
- record the approval or policy evidence before the operation;
- stop when the approval is denied, withdrawn, ambiguous, or unavailable.

MUST NOT:

- ask for a redundant approval before a routine operation covered by the
  current delivery authorization;
- infer authorization for a new issue, repository, branch, target, or
  out-of-scope change;
- treat a readiness flag as authorization without the task-scoped record;
- expand the target or effect silently;
- perform a hard operation under routine delivery authorization;
- treat `/address-pr-feedback` authorization as approval for a merge, rebase,
  review publication, or any target outside the validated feedback item;
- treat `/integrate-pr` lifecycle scope as approval for a fetch, rebase,
  force-push, merge, branch deletion, or worktree cleanup; its close-on-merge
  fallback is limited to the exact validated post-merge condition described
  above;
- treat `/implement-auto-issue` authorization as approval for review
  publication, rebase, merge, Ready-for-Review, or cleanup;
- treat `/refine-auto-issue` authorization as approval for issue create,
  review publication, rebase, merge, Ready-for-Review, or cleanup;
- treat `/auto-review-fix-pr` authorization as approval for check reruns,
  review publication, rebase, merge, Ready-for-Review, or cleanup;
- treat `/auto-ci-fix-pr` authorization as approval for review publication,
  rebase, merge, Ready-for-Review, cleanup, optional-check reruns, or treating
  pending checks as pass;
- treat `/ready-pr` authorization as approval for review publication, rebase,
  merge, cleanup, or a different pull request;
- treat `/plan-product` or `product-planner-agent` orchestration authorization
  as approval to create multiple GitHub issues or overwrite the parent issue;
- treat `/reprioritize-issues` or `issue-reprioritize-agent` orchestration
  authorization as approval to rewrite open-issue titles;
- treat `/close-issue` or `issue-close-agent` orchestration authorization as
  approval to close a GitHub issue, merge a pull request, or rewrite issue
  title or body;
- claim a policy-waived check passed when it was only waived.
